.TH HOGAR-SHELL 5 "" "hogar-shell 0.1.0" "hogar-shell"
.SH NAME
hogar-shell \- configuration file format
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I ~/.config/hogar-shell/config.toml
.SH DESCRIPTION
hogar-shell is configured in TOML. Every key below is optional; the default shown is what the shell uses
when the key is absent, so a working config is any subset of this page.
.PP
The file is watched and re\-read on save, and the change applies without a restart. A file under
.I ~/.config/hogar-shell/monitors/<output>/
has the same shape and overrides the global one for that monitor.
.PP
.B hogar-shell config schema
prints this same reference as a complete, valid config file, and
.B hogar-shell config schema <section>
prints one section of it. Both come from the running build rather than from this page.
.SH SECTIONS
.SS [active_window]
The `active_window` module. `compact` shows the app's class instead of the document title \(em stable while you move around inside one app, and much narrower.
.sp
Nothing bounds the title's length. The chip gives up width when its side of the bar runs short and the label elides, so a browser tab that runs to a paragraph costs the modules beside it nothing. A character count bounded it once and could not tell the two cases apart: it cut a short title on a wide bar exactly as readily as a long one on a narrow bar, which is the wrong unit for a question about room.
.sp
`inverted` puts the icon after the title instead of before it. Which reads better depends on where the chip sits: leading the icon points into the bar from the left, and trailing it does the same from the right, so a chip in the end zone usually wants this on.
.TP
.B compact
Default: \fBfalse\fR
.TP
.B inverted
Default: \fBfalse\fR
.TP
.B show_icon
Default: \fBtrue\fR
.SS [animation]
How the shell moves (`[animation]`).
.sp
Two curve families rather than one, because rsx has two motion models and they answer different questions. `curve` names a **spring**, for motion that chases a target that can move mid\-flight \(em the workspace indicator, which has to bend its path when you hold a workspace key rather than restart. `easing` names a **timing function**, for a transition with a start, an end and a duration \(em a panel opening.
.sp
`duration_scale` multiplies every duration at once, so "make it all a bit quicker" is one number; `enabled = false` collapses every duration to zero, which is the accessibility answer (and what a user on a remote desktop wants) rather than a per\-surface opt\-out.
.TP
.B curve
The spring preset for continuous motion: `gentle`, `snappy` or `bouncy`.
.sp
Default: \fB"gentle"\fR
.TP
.B duration_scale
Default: \fB1.0\fR
.TP
.B easing
The timing function for duration\-based transitions: `linear`, `ease\-in`, `ease\-out`, `ease\-in\-out`.
.sp
Default: \fB"ease\-out"\fR
.TP
.B enabled
Default: \fBtrue\fR
.TP
.B panel_duration_ms
How long a panel takes to enter or leave, before `duration_scale`.
.sp
Default: \fB180\fR
.SS [audio]
Audio control (`[audio]`). `increment` is what one wheel notch over the volume or microphone chip moves and what `hogar\-shell volume up` steps by. `max_volume` is the ceiling the sink can be raised to: PipeWire lets a sink boost past 100 %, which rescues a quiet laptop and wrecks a good speaker, so it belongs to the user rather than to a constant in the code.
.TP
.B increment
Default: \fB5\fR
.TP
.B max_volume
Default: \fB150\fR
.SS [automation]
What a command or an address a layout reads from may cost (`[automation]`): the `poll`, `listen` and `http` sources declared under a layout's `[sources]`.
.sp
It also sets how long the session actions wait on rules for the events that announce them.
.sp
Each source runs one command or request at a time, however many places read it. A run that fails is retried after a wait that doubles with each failure in a row, from `backoff_seconds` up to `max_backoff_seconds`, and is reported where `hogar\-shell layout check` and the problems notice list what a layout could not do.
.TP
.B backoff_seconds
How long a failing source waits before its first retry.
Range: 1 to 3600.
.sp
Default: \fB2\fR
.TP
.B max_backoff_seconds
The longest a failing source waits between retries, however many times in a row it has failed.
Range: 1 to 86400.
.sp
Default: \fB300\fR
.TP
.B max_line_kib
The longest line a source may print, in KiB. A longer one stops the command: a reading that size is not a reading.
Range: 1 to 1024.
.sp
Default: \fB64\fR
.TP
.B max_run_kib
The most one run of a `poll` command, or one `http` response, may print, in KiB.
Range: 1 to 16384.
.sp
Default: \fB1024\fR
.TP
.B min_interval_seconds
The shortest `every` a `poll` or `http` source may ask for. A layout asking for less is reported, and the source runs at this interval instead.
Range: 1 to 3600.
.sp
Default: \fB1\fR
.TP
.B shutdown_grace_seconds
How long logging out, rebooting or powering off waits for the rules that `logging_out`, `rebooting` or `shutting_down` triggers to finish their commands before it goes ahead anyway. 0 does not wait.
Range: 0 to 30.
.sp
Default: \fB3\fR
.TP
.B timeout_seconds
How long one run of a `poll` command, or one `http` request, may take before it is stopped and reported.
Range: 1 to 60.
.sp
Default: \fB5\fR
.SS [background]
Full\-screen wallpaper behind everything, one surface per monitor. Off by default so the compositor's own background shows through; setting an `image` \(em or `enabled = true` for a plain themed background \(em turns it on. `[background.monitors]` maps output names to per\-monitor images, each falling back to the global `image`, and `hogar\-shell wallpaper set` overrides both at runtime. Paths may use `~`.
.TP
.B enabled
Default: \fBfalse\fR
.TP
.B transition
How a change from one wallpaper to the next is drawn: `fade` (the default), `wipe` or `none`.
.sp
Default: \fB"fade"\fR
.TP
.B transition_ms
How long that transition runs, before `[animation] duration_scale`. Ignored while `[animation] enabled` is off, which makes every change instant.
.sp
Default: \fB600\fR
.TP
.B image
Unset by default.
.SS [background.monitors]
.SS [battery]
Low\-battery behaviour (`[battery]`): the levels that raise a notification, and the action to take once the charge is low enough that the machine should put itself away. On a desktop none of it ever fires, since there is no battery to read.
.TP
.B critical_action
A `session` action id \(em `suspend`, `hibernate`, `shutdown`; empty runs nothing.
.sp
Default: \fB""\fR
.TP
.B critical_level
Charge at or below which `critical_action` runs; `0` (the default) never acts on the user's behalf.
.sp
Default: \fB0\fR
.TP
.B enabled
Default: \fBtrue\fR
.SS [[battery.warn_levels]]
One charge level worth interrupting the user about, declared as a `[[battery.warn_levels]]` table. It fires once as the charge crosses down through `level` while discharging, and re\-arms once the battery is charging again \(em so a laptop left at 19 % does not warn every minute.
.sp
`title` and `message` left empty take the shell's own translated text, so the defaults follow the UI language instead of pinning English into everyone's config; `{level}` in either is replaced with the charge at the moment it fired.
.PP
The entries a fresh install starts with:
.PP
.EX
[[battery.warn_levels]]
critical = false
icon = "battery\-low"
level = 20
message = ""
title = ""

[[battery.warn_levels]]
critical = true
icon = "battery\-warning"
level = 10
message = ""
title = ""
.EE
.SS [bluetooth]
Bluetooth (`[bluetooth]`): the chip and the device list its panel shows.
.sp
`scan_on_open` is what makes "pair something new" one gesture instead of two \(em a panel opened to find a device starts looking, and stops when it closes, so no scan outlives the surface that asked for it. `show_unnamed` is off because a scan in a public place turns up dozens of devices BlueZ knows only an address for, and a list of addresses is not a list anyone can choose from.
.TP
.B enabled
Default: \fBtrue\fR
.TP
.B max_devices
Upper bound on the rows the panel lists, so a busy room can't grow the panel past the screen.
.sp
Default: \fB12\fR
.TP
.B scan_on_open
Default: \fBtrue\fR
.TP
.B show_unnamed
Default: \fBfalse\fR
.SS [brightness]
Backlight control (`[brightness]`): the step a wheel notch or `hogar\-shell brightness up` moves. Its own section rather than a key under `[audio]` so the per\-output and DDC/CI settings that follow have a home.
.TP
.B external
Control external monitors over DDC/CI with `ddcutil`, so a desktop with no backlight is dimmable at all. Costs one detection (a few seconds, on the service's own thread) at startup; does nothing when ddcutil is not installed.
.sp
Default: \fBtrue\fR
.TP
.B increment
Default: \fB5\fR
.SS [clock]
The `clock` module and its panel. `format` and `date_format` are `strftime` patterns, so a user can have anything from `%H:%M` to a full locale date without the shell enumerating presets; `twelve_hour` is the one switch worth naming, since it is what most people actually mean by "change the clock format".
.TP
.B date_format
Default: \fB"%a %d %b"\fR
.TP
.B show_date
Default: \fBfalse\fR
.TP
.B twelve_hour
Default: \fBfalse\fR
.TP
.B format
Overrides `twelve_hour` when set, for a user who wants seconds, a weekday, or anything else.
.sp
Unset by default.
.SS [clock.face]
How a face placed on the desktop is drawn, as against the chip these other keys dress.
.TP
.B background
Paint a plate behind the face, so it stays legible over a busy photograph.
.sp
Default: \fBfalse\fR
.TP
.B background_blur
How far the plate's edge is feathered into the wallpaper, in px. `0` gives a hard\-edged card.
.sp
Default: \fB0.0\fR
.TP
.B background_opacity
How opaque that plate is, `0`\(en`1`.
.sp
Default: \fB0.35\fR
.TP
.B invert
Draw the face in the theme's base colour instead of its text colour \(em for a pale wallpaper, where light text disappears.
.sp
Default: \fBfalse\fR
.TP
.B scale
Multiplies the theme's display size, so the face can be made as large as the screen allows.
.sp
Default: \fB3.0\fR
.TP
.B shadow
Drop a shadow under the glyphs, which is what keeps a plateless face readable over a light wallpaper.
.sp
Default: \fBtrue\fR
.TP
.B show_date
Default: \fBtrue\fR
.TP
.B format
Overrides `[clock] format` for the desktop face only. A desktop clock usually wants `%H:%M` where the bar chip wants seconds.
.sp
Unset by default.
.TP
.B date_format
Overrides `[clock] date_format` for the desktop face only.
.sp
Unset by default.
.SS [dashboard]
The dashboard surface (`[dashboard]`).
.sp
The two intervals are here rather than on the services they read because the cost is the dashboard's, not theirs: the performance cards redraw a sparkline per tick, and the playhead is an MPRIS property no player signals \(em following it means asking for it. A bar chip's own rate is unaffected either way, and neither ticker exists while the dashboard is closed.
.TP
.B avatar
The image the user card shows. Empty means the usual places \(em `~/.face`, `~/.face.icon`, then the AccountsService icon a display manager writes.
.sp
Default: \fB""\fR
.TP
.B first_day_of_week
Which column the calendar starts on: `monday`, `sunday` or `saturday`.
.sp
Default: \fB"monday"\fR
.TP
.B media_update_interval
Milliseconds between playhead reads while the media card is up.
.sp
Default: \fB500\fR
.TP
.B resource_update_interval
Milliseconds between resource refreshes in the performance cards.
.sp
Default: \fB1000\fR
.TP
.B tabs
Which pages the dashboard offers, in order: `dash`, `media`, `performance` and `weather`. An id it doesn't have is left out rather than failing the whole config parse, which would cost the user every other section over one typo \(em and is reported, by `hogar\-shell config check` and in the notice the running shell keeps up while a problem lasts. A list with no page it has left in it shows every page.
.sp
Default: \fB["dash", "media", "performance", "weather"]\fR
.SS [general]
App\-wide settings that don't belong to a specific visual section. `language` is a BCP\-47 tag (`"en"`, `"es"`); empty means "follow the OS locale, else English". `logo` is the icon the `logo` module shows; empty detects the distribution from `/etc/os\-release`.
.TP
.B language
Default: \fB""\fR
.TP
.B logo
Default: \fB""\fR
.TP
.B terminal
The terminal used to run a desktop entry marked `Terminal=true`; empty falls back to `xterm`.
.sp
Superseded by `[general.apps] terminal`, and still read when that one is unset \(em a config written before the section existed keeps working rather than silently reverting to `xterm`.
.sp
Default: \fB""\fR
.SS [general.apps]
.TP
.B audio_mixer
Default: \fB""\fR
.TP
.B browser
Default: \fB""\fR
.TP
.B editor
Default: \fB""\fR
.TP
.B file_manager
Default: \fB""\fR
.TP
.B media_player
Default: \fB""\fR
.TP
.B terminal
Default: \fB""\fR
.SS [gpu]
The graphics processor (`[gpu]`).
.sp
`backend` is `auto` (the default), one of `amd` / `intel` / `nvidia`, or `none` to switch the service off entirely. Forcing one matters on a laptop with switchable graphics: the kernel lists the integrated GPU first, and "the first card with a driver we can read" is then the wrong one. `card` names the `drm` entry (`card1`) directly, which is the only way to tell two cards from the same vendor apart.
.TP
.B backend
Default: \fB"auto"\fR
.TP
.B card
Default: \fB""\fR
.TP
.B enabled
Default: \fBtrue\fR
.SS [icons]
Where bar icons come from: an Iconify\-compatible HTTP endpoint (`{provider}/{set}/{name}.svg`) and the default set applied to a bare icon name. A name may override the set inline as `set:name` (e.g. `mdi:home`), so multiple icon sets work through one endpoint. `provider` is configurable because Iconify is self\-hostable/mirrorable. `app_icon_theme` names the freedesktop icon theme used to resolve notification app icons (empty = detect from GTK settings, falling back to `hicolor`).
.TP
.B app_icon_theme
Default: \fB""\fR
.TP
.B default_set
Default: \fB"lucide"\fR
.TP
.B provider
Default: \fB"https://api.iconify.design"\fR
.SS [idle]
Idle behaviour (`[idle]`): the timeouts, and what keeps them from firing.
.sp
`respect_inhibitors` is not a condition the shell evaluates \(em it selects which question is asked of the compositor. `ext\-idle\-notify\-v1` has one request that stays quiet while any client holds an idle inhibitor and another that reports raw input idleness, and the compositor is the only thing that can tell them apart.
.TP
.B enabled
Default: \fBfalse\fR
.TP
.B inhibit_when_audio
Hold every stage while something is playing audio \(em a film should not be interrupted by a lock screen.
.sp
Default: \fBtrue\fR
.TP
.B inhibit_when_charging
Hold every stage while the machine is on mains power.
.sp
Default: \fBfalse\fR
.TP
.B respect_inhibitors
Honour idle inhibitors taken out by other applications.
.sp
Default: \fBtrue\fR
.SS [[idle.stages]]
One idle timeout, declared as an `[[idle.stages]]` table: what to run once the seat has been idle that long, and what to run when it stops being.
.sp
Both actions are request lines the shell already answers \(em the same strings `hogar\-shell` takes on the command line \(em so a stage needs no new vocabulary and anything bindable to a key is bindable to a timeout. `hogar\-shell \-\-list` is the full menu; `lock on`, `shell dpms off` and `session do suspend` are the usual three.
.PP
The entries a fresh install starts with:
.PP
.EX
[[idle.stages]]
action = "lock on"
return_action = ""
timeout = 300

[[idle.stages]]
action = "shell dpms off"
return_action = "shell dpms on"
timeout = 360
.EE
.SS [keynav]
Keyboard navigation shared by every list surface (`[keynav]`).
.sp
`vim` is off by default and has to be: the launcher's list sits under a search field, and a list that reads `j` as "down" cannot also let you type `jitsi`. Turning it on is a deliberate trade a vim user makes knowingly \(em the arrows keep working either way.
.TP
.B vim
Default: \fBfalse\fR
.SS [launcher]
`fuzzy` off makes the query a plain substring match, for users who find fuzzy matching too loose. `hidden` lists desktop\-entry ids to keep out of the results entirely, and `favourites` lists ids to pin above the ranking, in the order given. `actions` are the `>`\-prefixed commands of the action mode.
.TP
.B actions
Default: \fB[]\fR
.TP
.B calculator
Show the calculator's answer above the app matches when the query reads as arithmetic. Unit conversions (`3 km in mi`) count as arithmetic.
.sp
Default: \fBtrue\fR
.TP
.B enable_dangerous_actions
Off by default: an action that can destroy something should take a deliberate opt\-in, not arrive with a config someone pasted from the internet.
.sp
Default: \fBfalse\fR
.TP
.B favourites
Default: \fB[]\fR
.TP
.B fuzzy
Default: \fBtrue\fR
.TP
.B height
Default: \fB420\fR
.TP
.B hidden
Default: \fB[]\fR
.TP
.B max_results
Default: \fB12\fR
.TP
.B qalc
Fall back to `qalc` (Qalculate) for an explicit `=` query the built\-in evaluator cannot answer \(em currencies, constants, dates. Silently does nothing when qalc is not installed.
.sp
Default: \fBtrue\fR
.TP
.B width
Default: \fB640\fR
.SS [launcher.icons]
Per\-application icon overrides, keyed by desktop\-entry id (`firefox`), valued as anything [`app_icon_view`] resolves: an icon\-theme name or an absolute path.
.sp
For the entries whose `Icon` key names something this machine's icon theme does not have \(em which is most self\-packaged software \(em where the alternative is editing a `.desktop` file the package manager owns and will overwrite.
.SS [lock]
The lock screen (`[lock]`): what it authenticates against, and what it shows while it waits.
.sp
The screen only comes up on a compositor that implements `ext\-session\-lock\-v1` and with a PAM library the shell can load. Both are checked *before* the lock is taken, because the failure mode of finding out afterwards is a user staring at a screen with no way back in.
.TP
.B fingerprint
Verify a fingerprint through fprintd alongside the password, when a reader is enrolled.
.sp
Default: \fBfalse\fR
.TP
.B howdy_command
The Howdy face\-unlock command, run with the user name appended; empty disables it. Exit status 0 is a successful match, as Howdy's own PAM module treats it.
.sp
Default: \fB""\fR
.TP
.B lock_before_sleep
Lock before the machine suspends, so the screen is already covered when it wakes.
.sp
Default: \fBtrue\fR
.TP
.B lockout_seconds
Default: \fB30\fR
.TP
.B max_fprint_tries
Default: \fB3\fR
.TP
.B max_howdy_tries
Default: \fB3\fR
.TP
.B max_tries
Attempts before the field locks itself out for `lockout_seconds`. `0` never locks out.
.sp
Default: \fB5\fR
.TP
.B media_detail
How much of what is playing a locked screen may show.
.sp
Default: \fB"title"\fR
.TP
.B notification_detail
How much of a waiting notification a locked screen may show. The summary and the body are never shown at any setting: a lock screen is read by whoever is in the room.
.sp
Default: \fB"count"\fR
.TP
.B pam_library
Where `libpam` is. Empty tries `libpam.so.0`, `libpam.so` and NixOS's `/run/current\-system/sw/lib/libpam.so.0`, which between them cover every machine met so far; set it only if `hogar\-shell lock status` says the library could not be loaded.
.sp
Default: \fB""\fR
.TP
.B pam_service
The PAM service to authenticate against \(em a file under `/etc/pam.d`. Empty picks the first of `hogar\-shell`, `swaylock`, `login` that exists, so a machine with no hogar\-shell\-specific stack still unlocks instead of refusing every password.
.sp
Default: \fB""\fR
.TP
.B trigger_on_wake
Attempt face unlock as soon as the lock screen appears, rather than only when asked.
.sp
Default: \fBfalse\fR
.SS [lock_status]
The `lockstatus` module: caps\- and num\-lock indicators. `hide_inactive` shows an indicator only while its key is engaged, for a bar that should stay quiet; off (the default) keeps both glyphs in place, muted, so the module is visible the moment it is added and the bar's width never shifts.
.TP
.B caps
Default: \fBtrue\fR
.TP
.B hide_inactive
Default: \fBfalse\fR
.TP
.B num
Default: \fBtrue\fR
.SS [lyrics]
Timed lyrics (`[lyrics]`).
.sp
Where hand\-kept `.lrc` files live is `[paths] lyrics`, with every other folder the shell owns \(em a file sitting next to the audio track is found without either. `online` is the only part of the feature that asks a third party anything (it sends the artist, title, album and length of what is playing to LRCLIB), so it is a switch of its own rather than part of `enabled`.
.TP
.B enabled
Default: \fBtrue\fR
.TP
.B online
Look the words up on LRCLIB when there is no local file. Sends the track's tags to that service.
.sp
Default: \fBtrue\fR
.SS [media]
The `media` module. `preferred_player` names an MPRIS bus suffix (`spotify`, `mpv`) to favour when several players are running \(em it only wins while that player is actually up, so naming one you don't always run never blanks the chip. `aliases` renames a player for display, since players name themselves badly often enough (`com.github.th_ch.youtube_music` \(-> `YT Music`) to be worth a config key.
.TP
.B marquee
Scroll a title longer than `max_chars` instead of cutting it. Off by default: a bar that never moves is easier to read past, and a marquee costs a repaint per step for as long as the track is playing.
.sp
Default: \fBfalse\fR
.TP
.B marquee_speed_ms
Milliseconds per character of marquee travel.
.sp
Default: \fB220\fR
.TP
.B max_chars
Max characters of `artist \(em title` on the bar, bounding a value with no natural size.
.sp
Default: \fB40\fR
.TP
.B preferred_player
Default: \fB""\fR
.TP
.B scroll
What the wheel over the chip does: `volume`, `track`, `seek`, or `none`.
.sp
Default: \fB"volume"\fR
.TP
.B seek_seconds
Seconds the wheel moves the playhead per notch, when `scroll = "seek"`.
.sp
Default: \fB5\fR
.TP
.B visualiser
Ring the dashboard's cover art with the audio visualiser. Costs an audio capture for as long as the media page is open \(em see `[visualiser]` for what the bars are made of.
.sp
Default: \fBfalse\fR
.SS [media.aliases]
.SS [modules."<id>"]
How a module is presented, whichever module it is: how a chip of it is dressed, and how big the panel and the hover card it opens are. `[modules.<id>]` holds one module's defaults, and an instance of it in the layout overrides any of them with an option of the same name.
.TP
.B drawer_max_height
The tallest its drawer grows before its content scrolls, in px. A maximum, not a height: a drawer with two rows in it is two rows tall.
Range: 80 to 1600.
.sp
Default: \fB280.0\fR
.TP
.B drawer_width
How wide its panel is as a drawer, in px.
Range: 160 to 1600.
.sp
Default: \fB320.0\fR
.TP
.B popout_max_height
The tallest that card may grow. Its transient is this tall whatever the card needs; the surplus is carved out of the input region, so it stays click\-through rather than swallowing presses.
Range: 80 to 1200.
.sp
Default: \fB300.0\fR
.TP
.B popout_width
How wide the card it shows while the pointer rests on its chip is, in px.
Range: 140 to 900.
.sp
Default: \fB264.0\fR
.TP
.B variant
How a chip of it is filled: `default` is transparent and highlights on hover, `filled` paints its accent.
.sp
Default: \fB"default"\fR
.TP
.B accent
The accent a chip of it draws with, over `[theme] accent`.
.sp
Unset by default.
.TP
.B open
Whether its panel opens as a drawer hanging off the chip or as a float in the middle of the screen. Unset is a drawer, except for a panel that is an application, such as the settings.
.sp
Unset by default.
.TP
.B float_width
How wide its panel is as a float, in px. Unset is 360, or what an application panel needs.
Range: 160 to 3840.
.sp
Unset by default.
.TP
.B float_height
How tall its panel is as a float, in px. Unset is 240, or what an application panel needs.
Range: 120 to 2160.
.sp
Unset by default.
.SS [network]
The network (`[network]`): the wireless list its panel shows.
.sp
`rescan_seconds` is a trade, not a preference: an access point that goes out of range emits nothing, so only a fresh scan notices it left. But a scan takes the radio off its channel, which on a busy link is a visible stutter and on some drivers worse \(em so the background interval is deliberately slow, and the moment that actually needs a fresh list (opening the panel) triggers a scan of its own. Turn it down only if a stale list bothers you more than the scans do.
.TP
.B enabled
Switches the NetworkManager layer off entirely: no D\-Bus connection, no rescan timer, no threads. The bar chip keeps working \(em it reads sysfs and never needed NetworkManager.
.sp
Default: \fBtrue\fR
.TP
.B max_networks
Upper bound on the networks the panel lists.
.sp
Default: \fB20\fR
.TP
.B rescan_seconds
Default: \fB300\fR
.TP
.B show_hidden
List networks that broadcast no SSID. Off by default: a hidden network cannot be joined by picking it out of a list anyway, so it is a row that can only disappoint.
.sp
Default: \fBfalse\fR
.SS [notifications]
Notification popups: what a card shows and how it behaves. Where the column sits, how wide it is, how many cards it holds and how long each stays are the column's \(em see [`StackConfig`].
.sp
The history panel's own behaviour lives here too, since it draws the same cards: `group_by_app` collapses an application's notifications under one header with a count, a mute and a clear, showing `group_preview_num` of them until the group is expanded; `action_on_click` makes tapping a card body invoke the notification's `default` action rather than only dismissing it; `body_lines`/`open_expanded` bound (or release) how much of a long body a card shows.
.sp
`sound` is a command run \(em detached, through `sh \-c` \(em each time a notification actually pops. Empty is silent, which is the default: a shell that started making noise on upgrade would be a bug, and the right command is per\-machine (`canberra\-gtk\-play \-i message`, `paplay /usr/share/sounds/...`).
.TP
.B action_on_click
Default: \fBtrue\fR
.TP
.B body_lines
Lines of body a card shows before ellipsizing. Ignored while `open_expanded` is on.
.sp
Default: \fB4\fR
.TP
.B critical_max_secs
How long a sticky `critical` notification waits before retiring to the history panel anyway, in seconds. `0` restores the unbounded wait.
.sp
A ceiling rather than a second timeout: sticky means "long enough that it cannot be missed", and the unbounded reading of that has one failure mode with no way out \(em a card whose only exit is a gesture, on a shell where the gesture did not land, stays on screen until the shell is restarted. Retiring is not dismissing, so nothing is lost when it fires: the notification is still in the panel behind the bell.
.sp
Default: \fB120\fR
.TP
.B critical_sticky
Whether a `critical` notification ignores `[stack] timeout_ms` and waits to be dealt with.
.sp
Default: \fBtrue\fR
.TP
.B fullscreen
Default: \fB"off"\fR
.TP
.B group_by_app
Default: \fBtrue\fR
.TP
.B group_preview_num
Default: \fB3\fR
.TP
.B open_expanded
Default: \fBfalse\fR
.TP
.B sidebar_size
How deep the notification centre is across its edge \(em the width of a left or right one, the height of a top or bottom one \(em in px.
Range: 240 to 1200.
.sp
Default: \fB400\fR
.TP
.B sound
Default: \fB""\fR
.SS [panels]
Panel behaviour shared by drawers and floating windows (`[panels]`): the gesture that opens one. How big each form is belongs to the module whose panel it is (`drawer_width`, `float_width`, ... under `[modules.<id>]` or on its instance).
.sp
**What is deliberately not here.** The gap a panel keeps from the bar is derived, never set: the bar's own outer gap when it floats, else a default so a hugging bar's panels still breathe. And its opacity is `[theme] opacity`, for every surface at once. Both used to be overridable per\-panel, and neither key bought anything but the chance for a drawer to sit at a distance, or at an opacity, that nothing else on the screen shares.
.TP
.B drag_threshold
How far a chip must be dragged away from the bar before letting go opens its panel, in px. `0` switches the gesture off. One threshold for every panel rather than one each: the gesture is the same everywhere on the bar, and a per\-panel distance would make the bar feel inconsistent under the same finger.
.sp
Default: \fB48.0\fR
.SS [paths]
Where the shell reads and writes user content (`[paths]`).
.sp
Every entry is empty by default, meaning "work it out": the wallpaper, screenshot and recording directories resolve through the user's own XDG directories, so they land in `Im\('agenes/Capturas` on a Spanish desktop rather than in a `Pictures` nobody has. `~` is expanded on read.
.TP
.B assets
Searched before the shell's built\-in assets, so one can be substituted without rebuilding.
.sp
Default: \fB""\fR
