Default: \fB["wifi", "bluetooth", "mic", "dnd", "game_mode", "vpn", "idle_inhibit", "settings"]\fR
.TP
.B window_preview_ms
How often the window info panel re\-captures its preview, in ms. `0` takes one still and leaves it, which is what a machine on battery wants \(em the preview is a screen capture per refresh.
.sp
Default: \fB1000\fR
.SS [visualiser]
The audio visualiser's *source* (`[visualiser]`): how the sound coming out of the speakers is turned into bars. Shared by everything that draws it \(em the desktop background, the media card \(em so the analysis is described once and the look belongs to each consumer's own section.
.sp
Nothing here starts a capture on its own: the service behind it runs only while something is subscribed, so a shell with no visualiser switched on never opens a stream.
.TP
.B bars
How many bands the spectrum is folded into \(em the number of bars every consumer draws.
.sp
Default: \fB48\fR
.TP
.B beat_sensitivity
How far above its recent average the bass has to jump to count as a beat. Around `1.1` finds a beat in almost anything; above `2` only the most obvious ones.
.sp
Default: \fB1.35\fR
.TP
.B floor_db
How quiet a band has to be to read as nothing, in decibels below full scale. Raise it towards `\-40` to make quiet passages flatter, lower it towards `\-80` to give them more life.
.sp
Default: \fB\-60.0\fR
.TP
.B frame_rate
Transforms per second. The upper bound on how often a visualiser surface repaints, so it is also the cost.
.sp
Default: \fB60\fR
.TP
.B gain
A multiplier applied before the bars are normalised. Above `1` suits quiet sources; the bars clip at the top rather than distorting.
.sp
Default: \fB1.0\fR
.TP
.B smoothing
How much of the previous frame each bar keeps, 0\(en1. Higher is smoother and slower; `0` follows the transform exactly and shimmers.
.sp
Default: \fB0.6\fR
.SS [visualiser.face]
How a row of these bars placed on the desktop is drawn.
.TP
.B accent
Draw the bars in the theme's accent colour rather than its text colour.
.sp
Default: \fBtrue\fR
.TP
.B gap
The gap between two bars, in px.
.sp
Default: \fB3.0\fR
.TP
.B hide_when_silent
Fade the bars out when nothing is playing, rather than leaving a flat line across the screen.
.sp
Default: \fBtrue\fR
.TP
.B opacity
How opaque the bars are over the wallpaper, `0`\(en`1`.
.sp
Default: \fB0.75\fR
.TP
.B radius
How round a bar's ends are, in px. `0` is square; half the bar's own width is a capsule.
.sp
Default: \fB3.0\fR
.SS [wallpaper]
The wallpaper library: which folder is browsed and how (`[wallpaper]`). The folder itself is `[paths] wallpapers`, so the two settings that name a directory stay in one place.
.TP
.B enabled
Whether the library is scanned at all. Off means `[background] image` is the only wallpaper the shell knows, which is what it did before the library existed.
.sp
Default: \fBtrue\fR
.TP
.B extensions
The file extensions counted as wallpapers, lowercase and without the dot.
.sp
Default: \fB["png", "jpg", "jpeg", "webp"]\fR
.TP
.B max_entries
How many images the library holds at most, so pointing it at a picture archive cannot cost a scan of the whole disk.
.sp
Default: \fB2000\fR
.TP
.B recursive
Descend into sub\-folders. On, because a wallpaper collection is almost always filed by theme or by artist.
.sp
Default: \fBtrue\fR
.TP
.B thumbnail_size
The edge length of a cached thumbnail, in px.
.sp
Default: \fB320\fR
.SS [weather]
The weather (`[weather]`).
.sp
`location` is a place name (`"Madrid"`), geocoded once; `latitude`/`longitude` skip that step. With none of them set the service asks an IP\-geolocation endpoint where this connection is \(em which is the only part of the feature that tells a third party anything, and setting either of the others avoids it.
.TP
.B enabled
Default: \fBtrue\fR
